{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c591","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a075ff-68a1-7c53-bd42-f28c0c61d002"},"sequence":291,"recorded_at":1780531400000577,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01319f54-880e-4865-b5c8-fc6dd7d16451","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"01319f54-880e-4865-b5c8-fc6dd7d16451","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"01319f54-880e-4865-b5c8-fc6dd7d16451"},"call_id":"call_01a075fff3257b73818effef0fd54cb9","text":"Read text file `internal/provider/provider.go`.\n1|package provider\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n5|\t\"crypto/subtle\"\n6|\t\"fmt\"\n7|\t\"path/filepath\"\n8|\t\"sort\"\n9|\t\"strings\"\n10|\n11|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/scalar\"\n12|)\n13|\n14|// Stable Structured Error codes this package reports. Discovery-shape\n15|// refusals (duplicates, malformed names, non-regular targets, unapproved\n16|// owners, relative directories) report invalid_config; a filesystem read\n17|// that cannot establish the discovery or verification preconditions reports\n18|// local_precondition_failed; a trust receipt that no longer matches freshly\n19|// read facts reports integrity_failure. The duplicate mapping is mandated by\n20|// Section 7.1; the other two are derived mappings documented on the\n21|// constructors, because Section 7.1 names no code for those cases.\n22|const (\n23|\tcodeInvalidConfig     = \"invalid_config\"\n24|\tcodeLocalPrecondition = \"local_precondition_failed\"\n25|\tcodeIntegrityFailure  = \"integrity_failure\"\n26|)\n27|\n28|// Error is the only error this package produces. Code carries the stable\n29|// registry code above; Detail names the offending provider ID, path, or\n30|// trust dimension in human text automation must never branch on; Cause\n31|// preserves the underlying filesystem failure for errors.Is and errors.As.\n32|type Error struct {\n33|\tcode   string\n34|\tdetail string\n35|\tcause  error\n36|}\n37|\n38|// Code reports the stable registry code for this refusal.\n39|func (err Error) Code() string { return err.code }\n40|\n41|// Detail reports the human-text detail for this refusal.\n42|func (err Error) Detail() string { return err.detail }\n43|\n44|// Unwrap exposes the underlying filesystem cause, if one was recorded.\n45|func (err Error) Unwrap() error { return err.cause }\n46|\n47|func (err Error) Error() string {\n48|\tif err.cause == nil {\n49|\t\treturn \"provider \" + err.code + \": \" + err.detail\n50|\t}\n51|\treturn \"provider \" + err.code + \": \" + err.detail + \": \" + err.cause.Error()\n52|}\n53|\n54|// The four refusal constructors below are declared as variables so the\n55|// refusal-inventory gate can observe every exercised refusal site. Each is\n56|// the single construction site for its failure class; production code must\n57|// not build Error values or wrap filesystem errors any other way.\n58|//\n59|// failDuplicate reports the Section 7.1 duplicate-ID refusal with the\n60|// section-mandated invalid_config code.\n61|//\n62|// failInvalid reports a discovery-shape refusal: a malformed plugin name, a\n63|// non-regular target, an unapproved owner, a relative directory, or a Trust\n64|// call on a builtin adapter. These are operator-configuration or caller\n65|// errors, so they share the invalid_config code.\n66|//\n67|// failPrecondition reports a filesystem read failure during discovery: an\n68|// unreadable plugin directory, an unresolvable symlink, an unreadable\n69|// target, or unattainable ownership metadata. A failed read is never a\n70|// legitimate absence, so discovery aborts with local_precondition_failed\n71|// instead of yielding a partial candidate set.\n72|//\n73|// failIntegrity reports a trust receipt that no longer matches freshly read\n74|// facts, including a receipt whose facts cannot be re-read at all: a\n75|// changed path target or digest requires renewed trust, and an unreadable\n76|// file cannot prove it is unchanged.\n77|var failDuplicate = func(providerID, firstSource, secondSource string) Error {\n78|\treturn Error{\n79|\t\tcode:   codeInvalidConfig,\n80|\t\tdetail: fmt.Sprintf(\"duplicate provider %q from %s and %s\", providerID, firstSource, secondSource),\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c593","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a075ff-68a1-7c53-bd42-f28c0c61d002"},"sequence":292,"recorded_at":1780531400000579,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01319f54-880e-4865-b5c8-fc6dd7d16451","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"01319f54-880e-4865-b5c8-fc6dd7d16451","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"01319f54-880e-4865-b5c8-fc6dd7d16451"},"call_id":"call_01a075fff3f87431906f2dc7704b6ad9","text":"Read text file `internal/provhost/surrogate.go`.\n1|package provhost\n2|\n3|// This file is the local lone-surrogate gate for the Section 1.6 wire\n4|// bodies this package decodes. Pinned specification Section 1.6 requires\n5|// decoders to reject lone surrogate code points before canonicalization,\n6|// and the repository already enforces that rule in\n7|// internal/canonicaljson (validateSurrogateEscapes, called from\n8|// decodeStrict). This package decodes with its own strict object reader\n9|// (decodeStrictObject in protocol.go) because it must keep member-level\n10|// fault attribution (duplicate member names, the offending member) that\n11|// the shared decoder does not report, and encoding/json silently replaces\n12|// a lone \\ud800-style escape with U+FFFD instead of failing. So the gate\n13|// lives here, scans the raw frame bytes before decoding, and refuses the\n14|// same class the shared package refuses.\n15|//\n16|// The gate is deliberately a copy, not an import: internal/canonicaljson\n17|// is byte-identical shared baseline text that two unlanded stories must\n18|// not edit at once, so neither story may add an export there. The\n19|// surrogate-vector sweep in surrogate_test.go drives both this gate\n20|// (through decodeStrictObject) and canonicaljson.Canonicalize over a\n21|// derived enumeration of the code-point space, so the copies cannot\n22|// drift silently: a divergence reddens there rather than passing\n23|// unnoticed. The sweep judges verdicts against the independent\n24|// implementation, never against a shared hand-written expectation.\n25|//\n26|// The gate decides the surrogate-escape question only. Malformed\n27|// escapes and unterminated strings are not its verdict: they fall\n28|// through to the JSON-syntax arms that already own them. Non-UTF-8\n29|// bytes never reach it: decodeStrictObject refuses them first,\n30|// because a raw WTF-8 surrogate encoding would otherwise slip past\n31|// this escape scanner and be silently replaced with U+FFFD by\n32|// encoding/json. A lone high surrogate is a high unit not immediately\n33|// followed by a \\uDC00–\\uDFFF escape; a lone low surrogate is a low\n34|// unit in any other position. Both are refused before any member is\n35|// read.\n36|\n37|// The gate's own range constants: the high and low surrogate halves\n38|// of the Section 1.6 verdict space. The\n39|// surrogate-vector sweep in surrogate_test.go derives its pair-second\n40|// dimension from these constants arithmetically (bounds plus and minus\n41|// small offsets), so the boundary neighborhood is enumerated from the\n42|// gate rather than from a hand-written literal: shifting a bound here\n43|// moves the swept neighborhood with it instead of silently uncovering\n44|// it. Verdicts still come from the independent implementation, never\n45|// from these constants, so a wrong bound reddens against the oracle.\n46|const (\n47|\thighSurrogateMin = 0xd800\n48|\thighSurrogateMax = 0xdbff\n49|\tlowSurrogateMin  = 0xdc00\n50|\tlowSurrogateMax  = 0xdfff\n51|)\n52|\n53|// hasLoneSurrogateEscape reports whether raw JSON bytes carry a lone\n54|// surrogate escape inside any string. The string walk mirrors the shared\n55|// package's walker (backslash handling included: a \\\\ escape consumes\n56|// both bytes, so the text \"\\\\ud800\" is backslash-plus-text, not an\n57|// escape), but only the surrogate verdict is returned.\n58|func hasLoneSurrogateEscape(input []byte) bool {\n59|\tfor index := 0; index < len(input); index++ {\n60|\t\tif input[index] != '\"' {\n61|\t\t\tcontinue\n62|\t\t}\n63|\t\tfor index++; index < len(input); index++ {\n64|\t\t\tif input[index] == '\"' {\n65|\t\t\t\tgoto stringComplete\n66|\t\t\t}\n67|\t\t\tif input[index] != '\\\\' {\n68|\t\t\t\tcontinue\n69|\t\t\t}\n70|\t\t\tindex++\n71|\t\t\tif index >= len(input) || input[index] != 'u' {\n72|\t\t\t\tcontinue\n73|\t\t\t}\n74|\t\t\tunit, end, ok := readHexUnit(input, index-1)\n75|\t\t\tif !ok {\n76|\t\t\t\t// Not a well-formed \\uXXXX escape: the\n77|\t\t\t\t// JSON-syntax arms own it, not this gate.\n78|\t\t\t\tcontinue\n79|\t\t\t}\n80|\t\t\tindex = end - 1\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
